  Sub-project 1 is small. Sub-project 3 is the one you asked for, and it gets much easier to specify once 1 and 2 exist.

  But there's a question sitting underneath all three that changes their size by an order of magnitude, so I want to
  settle it before we go further.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether this is a single-person tool running in one browser, or a real multi-user app.

  - [1mSingle-user, local[0m — tasks in localStorage, no server, no accounts. "Notified" then means [3mreminders[0m: due dates,
    overdue items, in-page toasts. Buildable in days. Rules out sharing entirely, and going multi-user later means
    rewriting storage and identity from scratch.
  - [1mMulti-user, real backend[0m — server, database, accounts, auth, sessions. "Notified" means the full thing you
    described: someone else edits a task, you find out. This is genuinely a multi-week project, and the notifications
    piece is the smallest part of it.
  - [1mSingle-user now, multi-user shaped[0m — local storage, but the data model carries user IDs and the mutation path emits
    change events from day one. Costs maybe 20% extra now, saves a rewrite later. Not free: you carry unused
    abstraction, and it can still be the wrong abstraction.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m the middle option only if you actually intend to ship this to more than one person. If you're not
  sure, take single-user local. Your original sentence said "users" plural and "tasks they care about," which reads
  multi-user to me — but that phrasing is also just how people describe software, so I don't want to infer a backend
  from it.
